WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.130 --> 00:00:04.713
Ransomware is everywhere.

00:00:04.713 --> 00:00:08.421
It's happening to the biggest companies.

00:00:08.421 --> 00:00:11.213
The cyber weapon NotPetya
started in Ukraine

00:00:11.213 --> 00:00:12.921
in June of 2017.

00:00:12.921 --> 00:00:15.671
It quickly spread,
paralyzing major companies

00:00:15.671 --> 00:00:18.588
and causing more than
$10 billion in damage.

00:00:18.755 --> 00:00:21.380
Government computers in 22 Texas towns

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:24.296
are being held hostage by ransomware.

00:00:24.296 --> 00:00:27.088
But it's also happening
at super low levels,

00:00:27.088 --> 00:00:28.713
where you have people

00:00:28.713 --> 00:00:32.463
ransoming individuals for
small amounts of money.

00:00:32.463 --> 00:00:34.963
And the thing that was most interesting

00:00:34.963 --> 00:00:37.463
and the thing that sort
of set us down this path

00:00:37.463 --> 00:00:39.838
is this thing called
ransomware as a service.

00:00:39.838 --> 00:00:41.713
And as soon as you hear that phrase,

00:00:41.713 --> 00:00:43.463
I mean, I want to read about that.

00:00:43.463 --> 00:00:46.963
The idea that people could
buy ransomware the same way

00:00:46.963 --> 00:00:51.421
they buy Salesforce
software or anything else.

00:00:53.130 --> 00:00:58.046
And so then we decided to send
Drake out into the dark web

00:00:58.046 --> 00:01:00.421
to procure some ransomware service.

00:01:01.463 --> 00:01:02.880
With a story like this,

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:03.963
all the reasons not to do it

00:01:03.963 --> 00:01:05.630
are actually the reasons to do it.

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:13.088
My name's Drake Bennett.

00:01:13.088 --> 00:01:16.088
I went on the dark web,
I bought some malware

00:01:16.088 --> 00:01:19.296
and I used it to attack and
extort my editor Max Chafkin.

00:01:20.630 --> 00:01:21.671
The original idea

00:01:21.671 --> 00:01:26.130
was just to do something about ransomware.

00:01:26.130 --> 00:01:29.755
The city of Baltimore was having this huge

00:01:29.755 --> 00:01:33.213
sort of battle with some hackers.

00:01:33.213 --> 00:01:35.671
Thousands of Baltimore city computers

00:01:35.671 --> 00:01:38.255
frozen by hackers demanding ransom.

00:01:38.255 --> 00:01:40.213
Baltimore's government computer systems

00:01:40.213 --> 00:01:42.380
recently faced a ransomware attack.

00:01:42.380 --> 00:01:45.171
Are you seeing these attacks
become more sophisticated?

00:01:45.463 --> 00:01:47.421
The more I learned about this world,

00:01:47.421 --> 00:01:49.630
the more frustrated I got.

00:01:49.630 --> 00:01:51.963
It seems hard to know
what you can trust here.

00:01:51.963 --> 00:01:53.421
There's a lot of anonymity.

00:01:55.505 --> 00:01:56.546
The more I thought about it,

00:01:56.546 --> 00:01:58.213
the more it seemed like

00:01:58.213 --> 00:02:01.630
it might make sense to try it myself.

00:02:01.838 --> 00:02:04.588
He wanted to do something participatory.

00:02:04.588 --> 00:02:06.796
It's really a cool way

00:02:06.796 --> 00:02:11.796
to explain a really
difficult technical topic.

00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:14.671
And then that also has the added benefit

00:02:14.671 --> 00:02:17.505
of testing out a hypothesis
I'd begun to have,

00:02:17.505 --> 00:02:19.838
which is that this
stuff has gotten so easy

00:02:19.838 --> 00:02:21.421
for a variety of reasons

00:02:21.421 --> 00:02:23.963
that almost anyone could
launch one of these attacks.

00:02:23.963 --> 00:02:24.796
And as it happens,

00:02:24.796 --> 00:02:26.671
I'd be a particularly
good guinea pig for this

00:02:26.671 --> 00:02:30.421
because I'm particularly
technologically illiterate.

00:02:33.338 --> 00:02:38.005
You got to have a hacker,
and that hacker despite his,

00:02:38.005 --> 00:02:40.963
I'd say modest computing skills,

00:02:40.963 --> 00:02:43.963
is Drake and the victim was me.

00:02:43.963 --> 00:02:46.255
And our idea was that
Drake could, you know,

00:02:46.255 --> 00:02:48.088
he's sending me attachments all the time,

00:02:48.088 --> 00:02:50.963
so the way we decided he was
gonna do it is he was gonna

00:02:50.963 --> 00:02:52.796
pretend to send me a draft,

00:02:52.796 --> 00:02:55.338
but that draft was gonna be ransomware.

00:02:55.505 --> 00:02:58.838
&lt;i&gt;- What were some of the legal concerns and &lt;/i&gt;
&lt;i&gt;how did you get around that?&lt;/i&gt;

00:03:01.296 --> 00:03:02.338
Okay.

00:03:03.005 --> 00:03:04.255
Legal concerns.

00:03:04.255 --> 00:03:09.713
What we figured out in
consultation with a very amused

00:03:09.713 --> 00:03:14.380
and maybe slightly confused
Bloomberg lawyer, was that--

00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:15.755
All of the laws that are on the books

00:03:15.755 --> 00:03:18.796
require not only the
possession of malware,

00:03:18.796 --> 00:03:21.171
but the intent to
actually launch an attack

00:03:21.171 --> 00:03:23.005
against an unwitting victim.

00:03:23.005 --> 00:03:26.380
Max, my victim was
complicit in the scheme,

00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:30.296
so we figured that kept us
on the right side of the law.

00:03:30.296 --> 00:03:33.421
And I do think there's a really strong

00:03:33.421 --> 00:03:35.546
public interest argument
for doing this kind of thing

00:03:35.546 --> 00:03:40.130
because if somebody as unsophisticated

00:03:40.130 --> 00:03:42.630
as a magazine journalist

00:03:42.630 --> 00:03:45.755
can get really dangerous ransomware

00:03:45.755 --> 00:03:47.380
without spending very much money,

00:03:47.380 --> 00:03:48.296
that's something that I think

00:03:48.296 --> 00:03:49.838
the public needs to know about.

00:03:54.088 --> 00:03:58.671
So once we kind of talked
to a Bloomberg lawyer,

00:03:58.671 --> 00:04:01.588
we then got two burner laptops,

00:04:01.630 --> 00:04:03.796
we got two cheap Dell laptops.

00:04:03.796 --> 00:04:06.005
Max and I both work for a company

00:04:06.005 --> 00:04:10.963
that takes data security very
seriously for obvious reasons,

00:04:10.963 --> 00:04:12.671
so we made sure

00:04:12.671 --> 00:04:15.671
that we kept all this
off Bloomberg's network.

00:04:15.671 --> 00:04:18.296
Then we decided to send him

00:04:18.296 --> 00:04:22.921
onto the dark web to procure
some ransomware service.

00:04:22.921 --> 00:04:25.380
So there are these dark web forums

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:27.338
that work sort of like they're chat rooms,

00:04:27.338 --> 00:04:30.755
but they're also these
kind of malware bazaars

00:04:30.755 --> 00:04:32.380
where you can go and people are hawking

00:04:32.380 --> 00:04:39.380
different forms of malware
and also different ways

00:04:39.380 --> 00:04:42.505
of getting that malware
onto computer systems.

00:04:42.505 --> 00:04:44.630
The market has now kind of advanced

00:04:44.630 --> 00:04:46.546
to the point where there
are these services,

00:04:46.546 --> 00:04:48.588
they're called ransomware as a service,

00:04:48.588 --> 00:04:51.338
and it's a play on this idea
of software as a service

00:04:51.338 --> 00:04:52.713
or SaaS, which is something you hear

00:04:52.713 --> 00:04:54.838
in Silicon Valley all the time.

00:04:54.838 --> 00:04:57.880
And so I found a couple,

00:04:57.880 --> 00:04:59.921
some of them turned out to be bogus,

00:04:59.921 --> 00:05:01.630
some of them seemed to be defunct.

00:05:01.630 --> 00:05:02.755
People just didn't get back to me.

00:05:02.755 --> 00:05:06.338
But there was one where
the guy got back to me

00:05:06.338 --> 00:05:07.755
when I got in touch with him

00:05:07.755 --> 00:05:09.630
and answered the few questions I had.

00:05:09.630 --> 00:05:12.463
And it was cheap, it was just 150 bucks,

00:05:12.463 --> 00:05:15.088
so I figured it was worth a try.

00:05:15.088 --> 00:05:18.213
So the first thing I did is
I reached out to the vendor

00:05:18.213 --> 00:05:22.171
and I used ProtonMail, which
is an encrypted email service.

00:05:23.130 --> 00:05:25.463
And at that point I had gone ahead

00:05:25.463 --> 00:05:27.880
and set up a Bitcoin wallet,

00:05:27.880 --> 00:05:33.838
so I paid the $150 that
was the subscription fee

00:05:33.838 --> 00:05:39.088
for the service and that gave
me a login for this website.

00:05:42.296 --> 00:05:45.380
And it was a pretty
simple looking interface.

00:05:45.380 --> 00:05:49.421
There was a series of tabs
at the top of the screen.

00:05:50.796 --> 00:05:55.255
One of the tabs took me to
the quote unquote dashboard,

00:05:55.255 --> 00:05:58.963
which is where I'd be able to
manage the various attacks.

00:05:58.963 --> 00:06:00.963
There was another tab

00:06:00.963 --> 00:06:04.130
that took me to what
was called the builder,

00:06:04.130 --> 00:06:06.588
which is a page that allowed
me to input a few pieces

00:06:06.588 --> 00:06:09.338
of information about the
kind of malware I wanted.

00:06:09.338 --> 00:06:11.713
Stuff like what kind of operating system

00:06:11.713 --> 00:06:15.505
would be on the target computer
or what kind of encryption

00:06:15.505 --> 00:06:18.005
I wanted or what's the email address

00:06:18.005 --> 00:06:20.713
that my victims should use to contact me

00:06:20.713 --> 00:06:23.171
once they realize they've been attacked.

00:06:23.171 --> 00:06:26.213
So I input those few pieces of information

00:06:26.213 --> 00:06:29.838
and it spits out a piece of software

00:06:29.838 --> 00:06:32.463
that I could then
download onto my computer.

00:06:35.630 --> 00:06:37.088
So it became obvious pretty quickly

00:06:37.088 --> 00:06:40.880
that I didn't have
particularly top shelf product.

00:06:40.880 --> 00:06:43.880
And that's not surprising.

00:06:43.880 --> 00:06:46.671
A lot of the conversation
on these dark web forums

00:06:46.671 --> 00:06:48.505
is about whether this or that product

00:06:48.505 --> 00:06:51.255
is reliable or how well it works.

00:06:51.255 --> 00:06:54.213
The person that we bought
the ransomware from

00:06:54.213 --> 00:06:56.421
turned out to be not
the most sophisticated.

00:06:56.421 --> 00:06:58.671
Almost as unsophisticated as we were.

00:06:58.671 --> 00:07:01.713
And it kind of started to become unclear

00:07:01.713 --> 00:07:05.130
whether he was trying to
con us out of more money,

00:07:05.130 --> 00:07:06.505
and I kept saying to him,

00:07:06.505 --> 00:07:08.046
"We got to be really careful

00:07:08.046 --> 00:07:11.171
that there's not an
additional layer to this scam,

00:07:11.171 --> 00:07:13.796
that he's not gonna ask us
to wire him some more money

00:07:13.796 --> 00:07:16.588
to make the software work better,"

00:07:16.588 --> 00:07:18.338
which is what he was trying to do.

00:07:18.338 --> 00:07:22.338
So there's just like so much con artistry.

00:07:22.338 --> 00:07:24.921
And there does seem to be a wide range

00:07:24.963 --> 00:07:29.963
in quality reflected partly
in the wide range of price.

00:07:29.963 --> 00:07:32.046
There are other ones that
are much more high end

00:07:32.046 --> 00:07:38.046
where it's not even an annual fee model

00:07:38.046 --> 00:07:41.796
it's more like you have a gang of hackers

00:07:41.796 --> 00:07:43.880
with different specialties

00:07:43.880 --> 00:07:46.088
and they just divide up
the pot between them.

00:07:49.796 --> 00:07:51.838
So I wrote my email to
Max, which basically said,

00:07:51.838 --> 00:07:55.755
'Hey Max, here's the
draft of my latest story.

00:07:55.755 --> 00:07:57.671
Sorry it's taken so long.

00:07:57.671 --> 00:07:59.255
The draft is attached'.

00:07:59.255 --> 00:08:01.630
Even though I had a really bad laptop,

00:08:01.630 --> 00:08:06.005
it immediately sniffed
out the potential

00:08:06.005 --> 00:08:08.671
that this attachment that
Drake was sending me,

00:08:08.671 --> 00:08:12.463
which looked super suspicious
to me, was going to do harm,

00:08:12.463 --> 00:08:14.755
and there were a bunch of
warning boxes that opened up

00:08:14.755 --> 00:08:18.171
saying, "Are you really sure
you want to load up this file?

00:08:18.171 --> 00:08:20.130
Are you super sure?"

00:08:20.130 --> 00:08:24.630
And of course I said yes,
yes, and infected myself.

00:08:24.630 --> 00:08:26.630
And then Max looked away for a second

00:08:26.630 --> 00:08:27.838
and looked back at his computer

00:08:27.838 --> 00:08:31.421
and there was this
ghoulish image of a hand

00:08:31.421 --> 00:08:34.588
reaching out from a cloud of
smoke and a message that said,

00:08:34.588 --> 00:08:36.796
"All of your files have
now been encrypted."

00:08:37.921 --> 00:08:42.046
And, so I was sitting there waiting
for this thing to happen.

00:08:42.046 --> 00:08:43.838
We had a photographer there.

00:08:43.838 --> 00:08:45.005
All of the documents,

00:08:45.005 --> 00:08:47.421
you know I had to load the
laptop with a bunch of documents

00:08:47.421 --> 00:08:49.880
that I wasn't afraid to lose.

00:08:49.880 --> 00:08:52.796
So I didn't have anything
important on there.

00:08:52.796 --> 00:08:55.505
And there's still something really scary

00:08:55.505 --> 00:08:59.921
about seeing that message on your computer

00:08:59.921 --> 00:09:03.005
that says that they own you now,

00:09:03.005 --> 00:09:05.171
that the attacker has your files

00:09:05.171 --> 00:09:09.755
and is gonna do with you what he wants.

00:09:09.755 --> 00:09:12.171
And it really makes you realize

00:09:12.171 --> 00:09:16.546
how easy it is to become a victim.

00:09:20.046 --> 00:09:24.046
The communities of people
who are in this world

00:09:24.046 --> 00:09:28.380
range from the most sophisticated hackers,

00:09:28.380 --> 00:09:30.796
so like, state-supported,

00:09:30.796 --> 00:09:35.380
connected to the military
or intelligence operation

00:09:35.380 --> 00:09:38.130
of some of the most powerful
countries in the world,

00:09:38.130 --> 00:09:43.713
all the way down to
literally a bored teenager.

00:09:43.963 --> 00:09:46.088
After all this was over I did reach out

00:09:46.088 --> 00:09:51.046
to my ransomware providers
and announce myself

00:09:51.046 --> 00:09:53.296
as a journalist and you know,

00:09:53.296 --> 00:09:56.838
they consented to be interviewed

00:09:56.838 --> 00:09:58.630
and basically what they told me,

00:09:58.630 --> 00:09:59.755
they didn't say much about themselves

00:09:59.755 --> 00:10:01.880
but they did say that they were a group

00:10:01.880 --> 00:10:06.838
of 18 to 26-year-olds with
different specializations

00:10:06.838 --> 00:10:09.546
working as a team to create this stuff.

00:10:09.921 --> 00:10:13.463
A lot of these chatrooms
where these products

00:10:13.463 --> 00:10:16.963
are bought and sold are
Russian language chatrooms.

00:10:16.963 --> 00:10:20.380
Some of them have actually
been geofenced or coded

00:10:20.380 --> 00:10:23.421
in such a way that they'll work anywhere

00:10:23.421 --> 00:10:26.713
except in places like Russia, Ukraine.

00:10:26.713 --> 00:10:29.088
But I don't know where particularly

00:10:29.088 --> 00:10:32.588
my providers were located.

00:10:32.671 --> 00:10:36.088
I also think it was
surprising in a good way

00:10:36.088 --> 00:10:39.463
that we really had to
work to make this work

00:10:39.463 --> 00:10:43.296
in terms of me ignoring warnings

00:10:43.296 --> 00:10:44.880
that my computer was giving.

00:10:44.880 --> 00:10:48.838
In terms of Drake having
to work with an expert

00:10:48.838 --> 00:10:52.380
to get the ransomware to work perfectly,

00:10:52.380 --> 00:10:55.380
but it's just kind of
like a scary reminder

00:10:55.380 --> 00:11:00.630
of how all of this kind of,

00:11:00.630 --> 00:11:05.630
there are all these sort
of bad actors and creeps

00:11:05.630 --> 00:11:10.421
and con artists kind of
lingering just below the surface

00:11:10.421 --> 00:11:13.921
of the internet and just
creepy how close they are

00:11:13.921 --> 00:11:18.838
and how you're not that far
away from downloading something

00:11:18.838 --> 00:11:21.421
that can kind of mess
up your digital life.

